---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="FAA & PSTAR Study Sources | PilotDesk"
  description="Find the official FAA sample questions and Transport Canada PSTAR study guide, with notes on using older sample material alongside current standards."
  canonical="/faa-sample-questions.html"
  robots="noindex,follow"
>
  <article class="sample-sources">
    <p class="eyebrow">Written exam study</p>
    <h1>Study from the source.</h1>
    <p class="sample-intro">Use the official question documents for the wording, figures, and current publication notes. The FAA and Transport Canada materials cover different certificates and rules, so keep the two jurisdictions separate.</p>

    <section aria-labelledby="faa-heading">
      <h2 id="faa-heading">United States · FAA</h2>
      <p>The FAA’s sample question PDFs are useful for seeing the style and subject areas of written exams. Some documents are older and may use standards or references that have since changed. Check the current Airman Certification Standards (ACS) and the document’s publication date as you study.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://www.faa.gov/training_testing/testing/test_questions" rel="noopener">FAA sample knowledge test questions ↗</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.faa.gov/training_testing/testing/acs" rel="noopener">Current FAA Airman Certification Standards ↗</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.faa.gov/training_testing/testing/supplements" rel="noopener">FAA testing supplements and figures ↗</a></li>
      </ul>
      <p>For practice that includes answers and explanations, go to <a href="/written-prep.html">PilotDesk Written Prep</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="canada-heading">
      <h2 id="canada-heading">Canada · PSTAR</h2>
      <p>PSTAR is Transport Canada’s student pilot permit knowledge requirement. Use Transport Canada’s current study guide and confirm the current rules in Canadian source material. FAA answers and standards do not apply to Canadian questions.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://tc.canada.ca/en/aviation/publications/student-pilot-permit-private-pilot-licence-foreign-military-applicants-aviation-regulations-tp-11919" rel="noopener">Transport Canada PSTAR study guide, TP 11919 ↗</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://tc.canada.ca/en/aviation/publications" rel="noopener">Transport Canada aviation publications ↗</a></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="study-heading">
      <h2 id="study-heading">A practical way to use sample questions</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Try the question before looking up the answer.</li>
        <li>Find the rule, chart, or explanation in the current official source.</li>
        <li>Check the question’s date and any referenced figure; do not memorize an answer whose source or assumptions you cannot confirm.</li>
        <li>Ask your instructor about wording that conflicts with current material.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Sample questions help with exam preparation. They do not replace instruction, current regulations, aircraft documents, or approved operational information.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <style>
    .sample-sources{width:min(780px,calc(100% - 2rem));max-width:none;margin:44px auto 80px;line-height:1.7;color:#d5d8dc}
    .sample-sources h1{font-size:clamp(2.2rem,6vw,4rem);line-height:1.05;letter-spacing:-.04em;margin:.5rem 0 1rem;color:#f5f6f7}
    .sample-sources h2{margin:2.5rem 0 .6rem;font-size:1.4rem;color:#f5f6f7}
    .sample-sources p,.sample-sources li{max-width:70ch}
    .sample-sources li{padding:.3rem 0}
    .sample-sources a{color:#c5e0d5;text-underline-offset:3px}
    .sample-intro{font-size:1.2rem;color:#bfc5ca}
    .sample-sources :focus-visible{outline:2px solid #b4d8c8;outline-offset:4px}
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
